    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5f56308d-7c89-565c-b22d-ca508f287be9",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left(x\\right)\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}+\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\cdot y=x^{3}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor.\n\nAssume $x>0$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we write the equation in standard form. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot y=x^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $Q(x)=x^{2}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =e^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & =e^{\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\,dx} \\\\  & =e^{\\frac{x}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)=e^{\\frac{x}{2}}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(e^{\\frac{x}{2}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot y\\right) & =\\left(e^{\\frac{x}{2}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(x^{2}\\right) \\\\ \\left(e^{\\frac{x}{2}}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{\\frac{x}{2}}\\right)\\cdot y & =\\left(e^{\\frac{x}{2}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(x^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(y\\cdot \\left(e^{\\frac{x}{2}}\\right)\\right) & =\\left(e^{\\frac{x}{2}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(x^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
